EXT. SUMMER CAMP ARCHERY RANGE - MORNING

Hay-bale targets. A rope line. Pine needles on dirt. Dew on the grass. Birds in the trees.

Six campers in a ragged line, each with a bow. Counselor-less. Waiting.

BIRDIE OKAFOR, 13, stands at the front, chewing her braid. FELIX BRANDAO, 14, a head taller, flexes a bowstring like it owes him money. MEI-LING CHOW, 12, tiny, squints at a distant target through huge round glasses. DARIUS WELLS, 14, in a Lakers jersey, sulks. ROXY MARCH, 13, spikes of dyed purple hair, polishes an arrow on her shirt. WALDO PRESTON, 12, plump, hugs his bow to his chest like a teddy bear.

BIRDIE
Where's Counselor Dave?

FELIX
Probably asleep.

DARIUS
Probably dead.

MEI-LING
He's not dead.

ROXY
How do you know?

MEI-LING
I saw him at breakfast.

WALDO
He ate seven pancakes.

FELIX
Then he's asleep.

BIRDIE
Should we start without him?

DARIUS
I'm not getting killed by a rogue arrow.

ROXY
You're more likely to get killed by Waldo's cooking.

WALDO
That's not fair.

ROXY
It's also not wrong.

Mei-Ling raises her bow. Squints. Lets go. The arrow wobbles, lands in the grass ten feet short.

MEI-LING
Oops.

FELIX
That's one way to do it.

MEI-LING
It's practice.

BIRDIE
Okay. Everybody. Same time. On three.

DARIUS ^
One.

ROXY
Two.

FELIX
Three!

Six arrows fly. Five hit the bale. One, Waldo's, thuds into the earth between Felix's boots.

FELIX (CONT'D)
Hey!

WALDO
Sorry!

Everyone laughs. Waldo hugs his bow tighter, red to the ears. A bell clangs from the mess hall. They lower their bows in unison.
